# Copy to .env.local and fill in. `npx supabase status` prints both values for
# a local stack; the dashboard has them for a hosted project.
#
# The publishable key is safe in a client bundle — it is the secret key that
# must never appear here.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sending mail from the hosted project. Nothing to do with the app bundle:
# these are read by `supabase config push` and never reach a client.
#
# Put them in `.smtp.local` — git-ignored, and deliberately not named
# `.env`-anything: Expo pulls every `.env*` file at the project root into the
# bundler graph and then fails trying to parse it as JavaScript.
#
# They must not go in `.env.local` either, which Expo loads into the app
# itself. An SMTP key in a client bundle is the ability to send mail as you,
# handed to anyone who downloads it.
#
# Any provider works; the free one that needs no domain of your own is Brevo
# (300/day), where the sender is a single address you verify by email.
#   SMTP_HOST=smtp-relay.brevo.com
#   SMTP_USER=<the login Brevo shows on its SMTP page>
#   SMTP_PASS=<the SMTP key, not your account password>
#   SMTP_SENDER=<the address you verified as a sender>
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
SMTP_SENDER=
